# The Levin–Abhinavagupta Synthesis

## How a universe of physical processes becomes a world of perspectives

My best answer is this:

> **A perspective appears when many competent processes become bound into a higher-order agent whose states matter to the continued existence and goals of that whole. Consciousness appears when this agentive organisation is not merely integrated but reflexively present to itself—when the system models the world, its own place in that world, and the fact that it is modelling.**

Michael Levin helps explain **how perspectives become individuated and scaled**. Karl Friston helps explain **how they maintain themselves, perceive and act**. Chris Fields helps explain **why every perspective is bounded and reference-frame-relative**. Abhinavagupta supplies what these scientific accounts still lack: a theory of **intrinsic presence, reflexivity, agency and manifestation**.

The resulting position is not standard physicalism and not an unqualified claim that “everything is consciousness.” It is best called:

# Reflexive multiscale agency monism

Reality is one continuous field of processes capable of forming nested, self-maintaining and self-interpreting centres. Viewed externally, these centres are physical and causal organisations. Viewed internally, sufficiently integrated and reflexive organisations are perspectives: lived presences for which events have significance.

The most compact formula is:

```text
physical interaction
        ↓
self-maintaining boundary
        ↓
goal-directed regulation
        ↓
integrated world-model
        ↓
reflexive self-location
        ↓
felt perspective
        ↓
coordination into larger agents
```

This does not prove Abhinavagupta’s metaphysics. But it gives us a far better translation between Tantra and science than crude statements such as “quantum physics proves universal consciousness.”

