# 3. The “Beautiful Loop”: from modelling to awareness

Active inference alone is too broad to explain consciousness. Simple physical systems can sometimes be described in variational or inferential language without plausibly being conscious.

A 2025 proposal by Ruben Laukkonen, Karl Friston and Shamil Chandaria attempts to specify additional conditions. Their “Beautiful Loop” theory proposes three relevant features:

1. a simulated world-model defining the system’s epistemic field;
2. competition and binding among inferences entering that model;
3. epistemic depth—the recurrent sharing of beliefs throughout the hierarchy.

The central idea is that the world-model comes to contain, nonlocally, information concerning its own existence and inferential activity. ([PubMed][6])

This is extraordinarily close to **vimarśa**.

A camera registers light. A thermostat controls temperature. But neither necessarily constructs a recursively integrated field in which the system’s modelling activity is itself implicated.

The Beautiful Loop suggests:

```text
model of world
      ↓
model includes embodied agent
      ↓
agent models its own modelling
      ↓
model influences future modelling
```

This recursion is not yet full autobiographical self-consciousness. It is a more minimal form of **field-level self-presence**.

The system does not need to think:

> “I, Thomas, am currently conscious.”

Its experiential world is already organised around an implicit here, now, possible action and anticipated future.

