# 4. Chris Fields: there is no perspective without a boundary

Chris Fields adds an essential correction.

A perspective is not merely a representation of an independently partitioned world. The system must actively establish and maintain:

* what counts as itself;
* what counts as environment;
* which variables matter;
* which distinctions it can measure;
* which reference frames allow signals to become meaningful.

Fields and Levin analyse cognition in terms of competency across arbitrary problem-spaces, while Fields and collaborators model biological systems as hierarchies of reference frames and active-inference processes. ([arXiv][7])

Fields’ work on contextuality and reference frames stresses that observation is never simply “reality as it is from nowhere.” Measurement requires physically implemented frames that distinguish outcomes, maintain standards and determine what can count as a signal. Contextuality is not merely an unfortunate subjective distortion added after perception; it is built into what it means for a bounded system to measure anything. ([arXiv][8])

This offers a powerful scientific analogue for **māyā**.

Māyā need not be interpreted as a hallucinated universe. It can be understood as the necessary process by which unlimited relational possibility is carved into:

* this perspective;
* these distinctions;
* this scale;
* this time horizon;
* this repertoire of possible actions.

A perspective exists by excluding most of reality.

> **To see anything is to fail to see almost everything.**

That is not a defect added to awareness. It is the condition for finite awareness.

