# 5. What Abhinavagupta adds

The scientific frameworks above can explain a great deal:

* boundary formation;
* integration;
* regulation;
* memory;
* prediction;
* action;
* emergent goals;
* multiscale agency;
* recursive modelling.

But there remains a gap.

Why should any of this be **present**?

Why is a recursively modelled colour not merely a control variable but a seen colour?

Why does tissue damage hurt rather than merely produce adaptive avoidance?

Why is there something it is like to occupy the model?

Abhinavagupta’s answer begins with the distinction between **prakāśa** and **vimarśa**.

## Prakāśa: manifestation or illumination

*Prakāśa* is the fact that something appears, manifests or shines.

It is not ordinary electromagnetic light. It is the basic disclosedness without which no phenomenon could be present.

## Vimarśa: reflexive apprehension

Mere manifestation is insufficient. Consciousness is not an inert illuminated screen.

*Vimarśa* is awareness’s capacity to apprehend:

* its object;
* itself;
* the relationship between itself and its object;
* its powers of knowing and acting.

Sthaneshwar Timalsina stresses that vimarśa is not passive self-reflection. It is active, synthetic and volitional: consciousness grasps objects while reflexively determining and integrating the modalities through which they are grasped. It is also inseparable from expressive potency or *vāc*. ([ResearchGate][9])

This is the decisive Śaiva insight:

> **Sentience is not simply illumination. It is illumination that possesses itself.**

A perfectly luminous but entirely nonreflexive field would not know that anything appears.

Vimarśa makes manifestation belong to a perspective.

## Svātantrya: freedom as constitutive power

Abhinavagupta does not understand freedom merely as choosing between options after the world has already been made.

*Svātantrya* is consciousness’s intrinsic ability to articulate, conceal, reveal, combine and transform its own manifestations. Timalsina describes this as an equation between freedom and power: consciousness is intrinsically active rather than a passive witness. ([Vimarsha Foundation][10])

> *citiḥ svatantrā viśvasiddhihetuḥ*
> “Free consciousness is the cause of the manifestation of the universe.”

This does not map neatly onto libertarian free will. A useful modern translation is:

> **Freedom is counterfactual depth—the capacity of an agent to model multiple possible states and reorganise itself or its environment in pursuit of them.**

A bacterium has little counterfactual depth. A planarian tissue network has more. A human can act toward imaginary states decades away. A civilisation can coordinate around futures no individual will live to see.

A recent active-inference proposal for “phenotyping” agency similarly operationalises degrees of agency through the structure of beliefs, preferences, explainable action chains and empowerment—the range of outcomes an agent can reliably bring about. ([arXiv][11])

The scientific measure is not identical to svātantrya. But it may measure its **finite, contracted expression**.

## Spanda: the dynamic act of manifestation

Dyczkowski emphasises that Spanda doctrine rejects the idea of the self as merely a passive witness. Absolute consciousness is an active force whose apparent stability contains continuous creative activity. ([Google Books][12])

Spanda should not be reduced to a measurable physical vibration at a particular frequency. It is the more fundamental alternation or tension through which awareness:

* opens into manifestation;
* differentiates its contents;
* gathers them back into unity;
* remains present through both differentiation and integration.

A scientific analogue would be the continual reciprocal movement between:

```text
prediction ↔ error
integration ↔ differentiation
self-maintenance ↔ exploration
global state ↔ local activity
model ↔ world
```

The similarity is structural, not an empirical identity.

