# 7. Ābhāsa: the world as model-conditioned appearance

Abhinavagupta’s theory of *ābhāsa* treats entities as manifestations or appearances within consciousness. They are not wholly unreal, but neither are they self-subsisting objects existing independently of manifestation.

Predictive processing offers a partial modern parallel.

The experienced apple is not simply raw external matter copied into the brain. It is an enacted perceptual hypothesis constrained by sensory exchanges:

* a bounded system predicts latent causes;
* signals correct the model;
* action samples further evidence;
* one coherent object becomes salient.

The apple as experienced is an *ābhāsa*: a determinate presentation shaped by the perceiver’s capacities.

But this does not entail that no world exists beyond human perception.

The stronger Śaiva claim is ontological:

> All objective reality is manifestation within consciousness.

The scientific evidence supports only the more limited epistemological claim:

> Every experienced object is model- and perspective-dependent.

The jump between these claims is where philosophy begins.

