# 8. The strongest metaphysical synthesis

There are three main options.

## Pure physicalism

Physical organisation somehow generates consciousness once it reaches sufficient complexity.

The problem is that complexity and function do not logically entail presence. An exhaustive external account of information flow still appears compatible with the question:

> Why should this flow feel like anything?

## Universal micro-consciousness

Every elementary component possesses tiny consciousness, and complex minds somehow combine these units.

This avoids emergence from complete nonexperience but introduces the combination problem: why should billions of micro-perspectives become one human perspective?

## Reflexive process monism

The intrinsic and extrinsic are two aspects of one process.

Externally, a conscious process is:

* integrated causal activity;
* model-based regulation;
* recursive self-maintenance;
* counterfactual control.

Internally, the same process is:

* presence;
* valence;
* temporality;
* perspective;
* reflexive awareness.

This is the position I find strongest.

It does not say that electrons secretly have human experiences. It says that the underlying reality described through third-person physics cannot coherently be assumed to be intrinsically devoid of every quality associated with presence. When processes achieve the organisation required for a unified, self-maintaining and reflexive perspective, their intrinsic character becomes a bounded phenomenal world.

Abhinavagupta expresses the intrinsic side:

```text
prakāśa + vimarśa + svātantrya
presence + self-apprehension + power
```

Levin, Friston and Fields express the organisational side:

```text
integration + boundary + goals + world-model + recursive control
```

The identity thesis is:

> **These are not two interacting substances. They are first-person and third-person descriptions of the same reflexively organised event.**

Loriliai Biernacki has already developed comparisons between Abhinavagupta and contemporary consciousness theory, including IIT, arguing that his system links subjectivity and materiality rather than treating matter as a wholly alien substance. ([MDPI][13])

I would extend this beyond IIT.

IIT concentrates upon intrinsic causal integration. Abhinavagupta demands more: integration must be accompanied by reflexivity, value, action and expressive power.

