# 9. What actually generates a perspective?

My proposed sufficient-condition stack is:

## 1. Operational individuation

The system establishes a dynamically maintained boundary through which it interacts selectively.

This does not require a hard physical shell. Boundaries can be physiological, informational, behavioural or social.

## 2. Normativity

Some states are better or worse **for the system**.

Without this, there is information but no significance.

A temperature change becomes meaningful for a cell because it affects viability. Valence begins where continued organisation is at stake.

## 3. Integrated regulation

Components do not merely act locally. Their signals contribute to control processes belonging to a larger whole.

## 4. Temporal depth

The agent retains effects of the past and anticipates possible futures.

Its present activity is therefore shaped by absent conditions.

## 5. A unified generative field

Perception, action and valuation are bound into one usable world-model rather than remaining disconnected reflexes.

## 6. Reflexive closure

The system’s model includes its own standpoint, uncertainty, agency and modelling activity.

This is minimal vimarśa.

## 7. Counterfactual freedom

The system can entertain and act toward multiple possible futures rather than being rigidly coupled to its immediate state.

This is finite svātantrya.

A system satisfying only the first three may exhibit basal agency without rich consciousness. A system satisfying all seven becomes a plausible subject.

There probably is no single magical threshold. Perspective may deepen as:

* integration increases;
* temporal reach expands;
* self-modelling becomes richer;
* value becomes more differentiated;
* alternative futures become available;
* the system gains control over its own attention and modelling.

