# 12. What the Neoplatonic stream contributes

Angela Voss’s work on Ficino emphasises imagination as an intermediary power rather than private fantasy. Images, music and symbols configure the soul’s relation to the larger cosmos. Ficinian practices attempt to alter the practitioner by establishing resonances between embodied imagination, affect, music and cosmological patterns. ([Cura][15])

Gregory Shaw’s interpretation of Iamblichus makes embodiment even more important. Theurgy is not simply an intellectual ascent out of matter. Material symbols, ritual acts and embodied participation allow the soul to receive and enact divine patterns it cannot generate by discursive reasoning alone. ([Academia][16])

In modern terms, these traditions recognise that a self is not transformed only by replacing explicit propositions.

Transformation can require reconfiguring:

* sensory environments;
* bodily rhythms;
* emotional salience;
* symbolic associations;
* social synchronisation;
* action patterns;
* attention;
* identity.

Ritual is a technology for modifying the whole generative model.

That does not establish that planetary gods literally transmit causal rays. But it explains why symbols, chants, postures and environments can have real effects: they perturb a multilevel embodied agent rather than merely its verbal beliefs.

Tantraloka’s ritual universe should be read partly in this light. Sanderson’s philological work also warns us not to extract a free-floating consciousness philosophy while ignoring the text’s location within Śaiva revelation, initiation, ritual technologies, lineage and competing tantric systems. The *Tantrāloka* is an immense synthetic exegesis of tantric traditions, not a disguised twenty-first-century neuroscience paper. ([Zenodo][17])

