# 13. Is the whole universe conscious?

Science currently supports three increasingly strong claims.

## Strongly supported

The universe contains physical systems that:

* form boundaries;
* regulate themselves;
* integrate information;
* pursue states;
* construct models;
* become reflexively self-aware.

The universe therefore unquestionably produces **local self-knowledge** through living and cultural systems.

When humans develop cosmology, biology and physics, parts of the universe construct models of wider processes from which they emerged.

## Plausible but unresolved

The intrinsic character of physical organisation may not be exhausted by its external mathematical description.

Dual-aspect monism, neutral monism and process metaphysics remain live philosophical possibilities.

## Speculative

The universe as a whole constitutes one unified conscious subject possessing intentions or experiences.

Neither Levin’s bioelectric research nor Friston’s formalism demonstrates a cosmic mind.

A system may contain conscious subsystems without itself being a single conscious subject. A forest contains organisms; it does not follow that the forest has one field of experience.

## The carefully stated Tantric hypothesis

The strongest defensible Śaiva interpretation is therefore not:

> “Scientific experiments prove that the universe is Śiva.”

It is:

> **Scientific findings increasingly show that matter is capable of intrinsic organisation, memory, goal-directedness, multiscale integration and recursive self-modelling. Abhinavagupta offers a coherent metaphysical hypothesis according to which these are finite articulations of a reality whose intrinsic nature is reflexive awareness and power.**

This hypothesis explains why agency and subjectivity appear progressively rather than arriving through a completely inexplicable jump.

But it remains a metaphysical interpretation of the science, not a conclusion forced by the experiments.

