# 15. A testable research programme

This theory should generate research rather than remain grand metaphysical prose.

## Measure the expansion of cognitive light cones

Alter cellular communication and determine whether collectives acquire:

* longer memory;
* larger spatial control;
* more distant target states;
* increased tolerance of component replacement;
* more flexible paths to the same outcome.

Levin’s bioelectric framework already provides experimental leverage over these questions. ([PubMed][1])

## Separate integration from reflexivity

A system may be highly integrated without modelling its own modelling.

Experiments should distinguish:

* causal integration;
* global availability;
* metacognitive monitoring;
* precision control;
* explicit self-location;
* counterfactual self-modification.

This would operationalise the distinction between mere *prakāśa*-like availability and *vimarśa*-like reflexivity.

## Test boundary plasticity

Meditation, ritual, synchronised action and altered body representations may change the experienced boundary of self.

Researchers can compare reported boundary dissolution with changes in:

* interoceptive precision;
* sensory integration;
* global connectivity;
* action attribution;
* temporal modelling;
* social synchrony.

## Quantify finite svātantrya

Agency should be assessed through:

* breadth of possible policies;
* depth of future modelling;
* capacity to revise goals;
* ability to recruit lower-level competencies;
* ability to resist immediate local error in pursuit of distant goals;
* empowerment over relevant states.

## Treat mantra and ritual as control interfaces

Rather than asking only whether a mantra’s theological claims are true, investigate how repeated sound, breath, posture, visualisation and group synchronisation reshape the embodied generative model.

The prediction is that effective rituals produce coordinated changes across multiple levels—not just conscious belief.

