# Final answer

What generates a perspective?

> **A perspective is generated when competent processes become causally and normatively bound into a self-maintaining whole whose integrated model is organised around one locus of possible action.**

What makes it real from within?

> **Its reality from within is the intrinsic aspect of reflexive integration: manifestation is not only processed but apprehended as belonging to the system’s own field. This is what Abhinavagupta calls vimarśa.**

Can perspectives form a world that progressively knows itself?

> **Yes locally and increasingly: cells form organisms, organisms form linguistic cultures, and cultures build scientific models through which cosmic processes become explicitly represented. Whether all of reality constitutes one unified cosmic subject remains unresolved.**

The cleanest synthesis is:

```text
Levin:
how smaller agents become larger selves

Friston:
how those selves infer and maintain their worlds

Fields:
why every world is bounded by a reference frame

Abhinavagupta:
why a genuine self is not merely integrated,
but intrinsically manifest, reflexive and active
```

Levin explains **how the One can become many organised centres without losing continuity**.

Abhinavagupta explains **why those centres may be internally luminous rather than merely externally functional**.

The scientific side gives us individuation without a soul-substance. The Tantric side gives us subjectivity without reducing it to dead computation.

Their combined proposal is:

> **Reality is a field of potential agency. Through constraint and communication, it individuates into finite selves. Through integration, those selves acquire larger worlds. Through reflexivity, the worlds become present. Through recognition, finite centres discover that their apparent isolation was a property of their boundaries—not the final nature of reality.**

